## Most-Violated Rules (always remember)

- **No network code** — no `URLSession`, no sockets, no analytics, no crash reporting. "Nothing leaves your Mac" is the product, not a preference.
- **No third-party dependencies** — `Package.swift` declares no `dependencies:`. Standard library and Apple frameworks only.
- **Never log or persist user text** — no `print`, no `os_log`, no translation history on disk. The translation cache is in memory, capped, and dies with the process.
- **4 spaces** for indentation, never tabs.
- **`@MainActor` by default** — every class that owns app state or an AppKit object is main-actor isolated. `HotKeyCenter` is the single exception among those (Carbon C callback; it hops back via `DispatchQueue.main.async`). SwiftUI views stay `struct`s, and static-only namespaces like `KeyDisplay` stay non-isolated — do not convert either into a class.
- **Settings only through `Store.shared`** — never read or write `UserDefaults` directly for a user setting. (The `didInitLoginItem` first-run flag in `AppDelegate` is the one pre-existing exception; do not add more.)
- **Never crash on a translation failure** — show the reason in the popover and carry on. No `try!`, no `fatalError`.
- **Easy Write reads the pasteboard and never writes it**, except in the single code path behind the popover's Copy button — `Clipboard.write`. A test enforces the count; do not add a second write.
- **Request no permission** — the app asks for nothing, and there is no `CGEvent` and no Accessibility check anywhere. Do not reintroduce a synthetic keystroke to make some later convenience work.
- **User-facing text**: English, sentence case, typographic punctuation (`’ “ ” –`), no emoji in app strings.
- **Never weaken the guardrail mode** — `.permissiveContentTransformations` exists because the default filter false-flags ordinary text for translation.

## Build & Run

```bash
swift build                 # debug
swift build -c release      # what build.sh compiles
./test.sh                   # unit tests (wraps `swift test`; needed when only the CLT are installed)
./build.sh                  # release build → EasyWrite.app bundle → codesign
./setup-signing.sh          # optional, one-time: stable self-signed identity
```

Builds only on macOS 26+ / Apple Silicon. The suite covers `EasyWriteCore` and the single-pasteboard-write invariant and nothing else — see `docs/conventions/testing/README.md` before claiming coverage. There is no linter and no CI; `swift build` must finish warning-free.

## Git

- Commit format: a capitalised English sentence describing the change, no prefix tag, no trailing period. Examples from real history: "Add Arabic language (v1.1)", "Show specific reason when Apple Intelligence is unavailable", "Replace README demo with a premium animated walkthrough".
- Branches: `dev/<descriptive-name>` (e.g. `dev/popup`). `main` is the primary branch.
- Never commit `.build/`, `EasyWrite.app/`, `Package.resolved`, `.swiftpm/`, or `.DS_Store` (see `.gitignore`). Signing keys live in a keychain under `~/Library`, never in the repo.

## Versioning

A user-visible release bumps `CFBundleShortVersionString` **and** `CFBundleVersion` in `Info.plist`, and adds a `CHANGELOG.md` entry. The popover's gear menu reads the short version straight from the bundle.

